She points right at Jim and says, but I didn't get the job.

741
00:33:11.980 --> 00:33:15.640
And apparently she got really upset about this and went on a shopping spree.

742
00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:17.900
She bought 13 pianos.

743
00:33:17.900 --> 00:33:19.320
So all of this,

744
00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:30.100
when she's explaining her breakdown and how she bought the pianos and then how she had this epiphany about selling things and she went and talked to Joe and then she got the job.

745
00:33:30.260 --> 00:33:38.480
It was so well done that I went to the script because I wanted to know like how much of it was scripted and maybe did Catherine improvise.

746
00:33:38.480 --> 00:33:41.520
Because it was scripted word for word.

747
00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:43.320
That is how good she is.

748
00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:47.080
I thought she was literally coming up with half that stuff on the fly.

749
00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:53.540
That just shows, I feel like, when we have good writing and then a great performer, how just magic.

750
00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:57.800
Watching Catherine in this scene, I totally lost where I was.

751
00:33:58.000 --> 00:33:58.360
Mm-hmm.

752
00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:05.580
And if I had been in that conference room, I would have forgotten to act because I was just so enthralled with her performance.

753
00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:08.820
I wish it would have gone on another five minutes.

754
00:34:08.860 --> 00:34:11.260
Like, I could have watched even more of that.

755
00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:19.240
Well, lady, I think we should take a break before we get too deep into Dwight's appendicitis and Andy's obsession with reception.

756
00:34:19.500 --> 00:34:19.900
Mm-hmm.

757
00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:27.100
And when we come back, I actually have some interesting details on how this episode was received by fans and critics.

758
00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:28.980
There was a lot of hubba-bub.

759
00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:31.100
Did Miles McNutt write about it?

760
00:34:31.280 --> 00:34:33.400
He did, and I'm going to tell you what he said.

761
00:34:33.660 --> 00:34:33.960
Okay.

762
00:34:33.960 --> 00:34:45.700
All right, we are back.

763
00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:54.140
And before we keep breaking down this episode, I thought maybe you might be interested to know how this episode, Tallahassee, was received.

764
00:34:54.380 --> 00:34:55.680
I am so interested.

765
00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:56.720
All right.

766
00:34:57.020 --> 00:34:58.420
It was very mixed.

767
00:34:58.660 --> 00:34:59.000
Hmm.

768
00:34:59.240 --> 00:35:04.260
But it was sort of everybody versus Miles McNutt.

769
00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:05.080
Oh, yeah?

770
00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:15.620
Okay, so TVEquals did a review of this episode and said it was one of the best episodes of the season and maybe even of the last couple of seasons.

771
00:35:15.900 --> 00:35:16.160
Wow.

772
00:35:16.160 --> 00:35:27.340
Among Office Tally fans, Tallahassee was ranked number one for season eight, the number one episode, most favorite among fans on Office Tally.

773
00:35:27.520 --> 00:35:28.960
Of all of season eight?

774
00:35:29.060 --> 00:35:30.160
Of all of season eight.

775
00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:34.840
However, Miles McNutt, not a fan.

776
00:35:35.620 --> 00:35:36.840
Gave it a C minus.

777
00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:37.540
Hmm.

778
00:35:38.700 --> 00:35:39.640
Here's a quote.

779
00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:46.580
While I left last week's episode with a sense of hope, Tallahassee has lived up to none of that potential.

780
00:35:46.840 --> 00:35:47.660
Oh, gosh.

781
00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:52.900
And you can imagine the rest of the review from there.

782
00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:53.160
Yeah.

783
00:35:53.820 --> 00:35:55.480
Well, he got some pushback.

784
00:35:55.920 --> 00:35:57.840
A fan named Steve B.

785
00:35:58.020 --> 00:36:00.500
wrote on the comment board, quote,

786
00:36:00.720 --> 00:36:04.100
I think you need to recuse yourself from reviewing this show.

787
00:36:04.560 --> 00:36:07.820
Each week, you find a new reason to hate a particular episode.

788
00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:11.500
When the show gives you what you want, you find another reason.

789
00:36:11.760 --> 00:36:17.200
For the last two seasons, you've been hitting the drumbeat of character-based comedy pretty hard.

790
00:36:17.200 --> 00:36:26.180
Yet, here we have an episode that hinges largely on Dwight's characterization, his belligerence, ambition, paranoia, and contempt for the common man.

791
00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:28.280
And it was a funny episode.

792
00:36:28.660 --> 00:36:29.820
Well, guess what?

793
00:36:30.040 --> 00:36:30.380
What?

794
00:36:30.760 --> 00:36:36.520
In his review for After Hours, which is next week's episode, Miles McNutt said this.

795
00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:38.940
Did he address Steve B.?

796
00:36:38.940 --> 00:36:41.560
Well, sort of.

797
00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:43.600
Miles said, quote,

798
00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:48.060
Last week's episode probably should have been above a C-minus.

799
00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:48.500
Oh.

800
00:36:48.780 --> 00:36:51.600
But I went with my gut, and I have to stand by that decision.

801
00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:52.860
Go Miles.

802
00:36:53.180 --> 00:36:56.380
I support him standing by his first reaction.

803
00:36:56.700 --> 00:36:58.260
Yes, that was his first reaction.

804
00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:06.380
I'll tell you, among fans, generally they loved this episode, but the cold open was hotly debated.

805
00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:09.640
There was a huge lover hate of the cold open.

806
00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:12.140
We got a letter from Dennis D.

807
00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:15.060
in Tallahassee, Florida, who said,

808
00:37:15.240 --> 00:37:19.740
My favorite prank in the show is this episode's cold open.

809
00:37:20.020 --> 00:37:23.980
But the message boards regarding this cold open, when the show aired,

810
00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:30.640
one person said they deleted the episode from their DVR after watching the cold open because they hated it so much.

811
00:37:30.980 --> 00:37:31.120
Wow.

812
00:37:31.120 --> 00:37:34.880
But then other people said it was the funniest cold open in a long time.

813
00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:35.920
They agreed with Dennis.

814
00:37:36.020 --> 00:37:37.300
You can't make everybody happy.

815
00:37:37.440 --> 00:37:37.940
You can't.

816
00:37:38.060 --> 00:37:41.180
You especially can't make Miles McNutt happy very easily.

817
00:37:41.440 --> 00:37:46.000
I have to wonder, and I'm sure it's not, is Steve B.

818
00:37:46.160 --> 00:37:47.300
Steve Burgess?

819
00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:48.740
Wait.

820
00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:50.360
Wait.

821
00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:52.120
That is so funny.

822
00:37:52.300 --> 00:37:55.060
Did Steve Burgess go on Miles McNutt's review?

823
00:37:55.360 --> 00:37:59.400
I'm sure he did it because he's busy and he probably never would have done that.

824
00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:02.700
But it is kind of funny that it's Steve B.

825
00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:04.940
I didn't even think of that, Angela.

826
00:38:05.180 --> 00:38:06.700
I kind of want to ask Steve.

827
00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:07.340
I know.

828
00:38:07.540 --> 00:38:08.000
I do, too.

829
00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:11.440
Did you ever write on Miles McNutt's comment board?

830
00:38:11.960 --> 00:38:13.620
Oh, I love that so much.

831
00:38:13.620 --> 00:38:16.160
And ask him to recuse himself.

832
00:38:19.060 --> 00:38:24.840
Well, I want to point out this next scene because, you know, Dwight is really popping the antacids now.

833
00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:26.300
He's in major pain.

834
00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:26.880
He is.

835
00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:31.460
And Nellie is going to ask someone to help with the projection screen to pull it down.

836
00:38:31.640 --> 00:38:33.680
She points out that Ryan couldn't reach it.

837
00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:35.440
It would be so funny if he tried.

838
00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:36.280
My balloon.

839
00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:43.900
And it's in this moment that Dwight's like, I must appear like a strong, tall leader.

840
00:38:44.200 --> 00:38:46.260
Especially because she asked Jim to do it, right?

841
00:38:46.260 --> 00:38:52.420
I have to say, Rainn Wilson completely crushed it.

842
00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:57.580
The way he sold his pain as he reaches up for that screen to pull it down.

843
00:38:58.360 --> 00:39:00.680
I thought he was brilliant in this whole episode.

844
00:39:00.980 --> 00:39:03.680
The way he portrayed his pain escalating.

845
00:39:03.980 --> 00:39:06.340
I honestly, Rainn, oh my God.

846
00:39:06.520 --> 00:39:08.300
You were so great in this episode.

847
00:39:08.780 --> 00:39:10.900
I thought you should have won an Emmy for playing Dwight.

848
00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:12.560
He was nominated three times.

849
00:39:12.860 --> 00:39:14.100
You and John were nominated.

850
00:39:14.400 --> 00:39:16.460
Steve, none of you guys won an Emmy.

851
00:39:16.700 --> 00:39:18.260
You all should have won Emmys.

852
00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:18.920
Wow.

853
00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:20.580
This has taken a turn.

854
00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:21.320
This speech.

855
00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:25.220
It's turned into a We Deserve Emmys speech because of the pulling down of the screen.

856
00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:25.960
I love it.

857
00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:27.200
So many other moments.

858
00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:31.620
And you know, you were sitting right by me when Steve didn't win and it was his last

859
00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:34.680
time nominated and I couldn't control myself and I said a curse word.

860
00:39:34.760 --> 00:39:35.240
You did.

861
00:39:35.420 --> 00:39:36.480
With the camera right by me.

862
00:39:36.580 --> 00:39:36.740
Yeah.

863
00:39:37.960 --> 00:39:40.420
I just thought Rainn should have won an Emmy.

864
00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:43.280
Well, you know who agrees with you is Kelly M.

865
00:39:43.480 --> 00:39:44.940
from Woodbridge, Virginia.

866
00:39:45.860 --> 00:39:50.920
Kellyanne wrote in and said, I have been waiting for so long to submit this to you ladies.

867
00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:54.220
I thought I would save you some time on a deep dive.

868
00:39:54.220 --> 00:40:00.500
I am a pediatric RN in Minnesota and I work with kids with appendicitis nearly every shift.

869
00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:02.800
I have also had my own appendix removed.

870
00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:03.400
Oh, wow.

871
00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:09.440
The most distinct symptom is when we as medical personnel push slightly on someone's abdomen

872
00:40:09.440 --> 00:40:14.700
and the most pain is actually when we release the pressure, not when we apply the pressure.

873
00:40:14.900 --> 00:40:17.140
We call this rebound tenderness.

874
00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:22.880
Appendicitis is a serious medical issue and there were a few moments that stuck out to me in this

875
00:40:22.880 --> 00:40:25.880
episode that shows how well Rainn Wilson performed.

876
00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:29.540
First is the way he walks and stands through the whole episode.

877
00:40:29.700 --> 00:40:34.900
As soon as he walked out of the hotel, I saw the antacids and the hunched over walk and I had a

878
00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:36.060
feeling it was appendicitis.

879
00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:41.560
When he reached for the projector screen, it was so uncomfortable but also so funny because I knew

880
00:40:41.560 --> 00:40:47.380
exactly how that felt because standing up straight hurts so bad when you have appendicitis.

881
00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:51.720
And then, of course, he goes on to show more symptoms and more severe pain.

882
00:40:51.940 --> 00:40:55.740
I have to give huge props to Rainn Wilson for his spot-on performance.

883
00:40:55.740 --> 00:40:57.320
It was just so believable.

884
00:40:57.740 --> 00:41:03.020
His gradual progression of symptoms throughout the episode is perfect and I loved every minute of it.

885
00:41:03.460 --> 00:41:08.960
Also, applause to Daniel Chun and the writing staff for doing their research to make this so realistic.

886
00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:11.240
Kellyanne, thank you for writing in.

887
00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:16.360
You're clearly an expert on appendicitis and I'm so glad we did a good portrayal of this.

888
00:41:16.360 --> 00:41:17.200
Me too!

889
00:41:17.500 --> 00:41:21.740
From the writing to Rainn's performance, which deserved an Emmy.

890
00:41:22.020 --> 00:41:22.280
Yes.

891
00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:26.920
Dwight has finally had enough with the pain and he has called, I guess, 911?

892
00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:29.080
I don't know who he's called.

893
00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:36.560
He's called someone in the medical profession and he's saying he has symptoms that might mimic being poisoned or appendicitis.

894
00:41:37.020 --> 00:41:38.760
Jim's like, Dwight, I didn't poison you.

895
00:41:39.020 --> 00:41:39.360
Yeah.

896
00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:42.420
And that's when Dwight's like, get me an ambulance right away.

897
00:41:42.420 --> 00:41:50.320
Until he discovers that Nellie needs someone in the position of vice president of special projects and then he gets off the phone.

898
00:41:50.820 --> 00:41:50.960
Yeah.

899
00:41:51.240 --> 00:41:58.540
Dwight has a phrase I am now going to adopt into my life and I'm going to say it whenever I want, especially if I'm agitated.

900
00:41:58.800 --> 00:41:59.560
What is it?

901
00:41:59.780 --> 00:42:01.820
Send it to the freaking moon, idiot.

902
00:42:02.380 --> 00:42:02.860
Okay.

903
00:42:02.920 --> 00:42:04.060
Oh my goodness.

904
00:42:04.340 --> 00:42:06.340
That's how he cancels the ambulance.

905
00:42:06.620 --> 00:42:06.760
Yeah.

906
00:42:06.760 --> 00:42:08.640
They say, where should I send the ambulance?

907
00:42:08.740 --> 00:42:10.740
And he says, send it to the freaking moon, idiot.

908
00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:12.360
That's what I'm going to say now.

909
00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:13.300
Mm-hmm.

910
00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:14.620
That's going to be my new phrase.

911
00:42:15.080 --> 00:42:21.980
After canceling the ambulance, he's going to saddle up to Nellie, who's having a conversation with Todd Packer about hunting.

912
00:42:22.340 --> 00:42:27.520
And this is when Dwight is going to explain that he is a master hunter, but only a decent baiter.

913
00:42:28.340 --> 00:42:31.640
Yes, because Todd Packer's trying to mess with him.

914
00:42:31.760 --> 00:42:33.480
He says, did you say master baiter?

915
00:42:33.480 --> 00:42:37.600
And he goes, no, I'm a decent baiter, but my cousin Moe's, that's a master baiter.

916
00:42:37.780 --> 00:42:38.040
Mm-hmm.

917
00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:42.720
Next up, for some reason, Nellie is going to suggest everyone make a human pyramid.

918
00:42:43.340 --> 00:42:44.780
This is very Michael Scott.

919
00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:46.060
It is.

920
00:42:46.220 --> 00:42:46.560
Mm-hmm.

921
00:42:46.760 --> 00:42:47.380
But guess what?

922
00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:51.160
Florida Stanley is up for it, which means then Jim is up for it.

923
00:42:51.460 --> 00:42:51.510
Yeah.

924
00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:53.880
Aaron's like, sure, I'll do it.

925
00:42:54.560 --> 00:42:58.740
Steve Burgess said that our stunt coordinator, Brett Jones, had to plan this whole pyramid.

926
00:42:58.740 --> 00:43:03.280
And we interviewed background actors specifically to be in the pyramid.

927
00:43:03.680 --> 00:43:09.280
So, like, you knew from day one, eventually you're going to get in a human pyramid.

928
00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:12.020
They also rigged a safety line for Rain.

929
00:43:12.260 --> 00:43:12.580
Uh-huh.

930
00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:14.200
For him to crawl up to the top?

931
00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:16.880
Yes, but they can take out in post, you know?

932
00:43:17.220 --> 00:43:21.700
And I don't know if you noticed, but Leslie is not on the bottom of that pyramid.

933
00:43:21.920 --> 00:43:26.760
We had a stunt person dressed as Stanley who stood in.

934
00:43:26.760 --> 00:43:27.280
Hmm.

935
00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:29.520
Well, this scene was actually bigger.

936
00:43:29.820 --> 00:43:35.100
There was a moment where Aaron makes some small talk with a woman named Stacy, who is

937
00:43:35.100 --> 00:43:38.340
from Tallahassee, who's at this meeting.

938
00:43:38.680 --> 00:43:41.900
And Stacy says to Aaron, wow, you're moving here for good.

939
00:43:42.100 --> 00:43:45.220
Aaron says, yeah, I've never lived in a state capitol before.

940
00:43:45.420 --> 00:43:46.740
It seems super exciting.

941
00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:50.340
Stacy says, well, Tallahassee's glad to have you.

942
00:43:50.480 --> 00:43:52.600
So what are your main priorities in a place?

943
00:43:52.760 --> 00:43:53.480
Cheap rent?

944
00:43:53.960 --> 00:43:54.700
Artsy neighborhood?

945
00:43:55.060 --> 00:43:55.620
Big yard?

946
00:43:56.140 --> 00:44:01.400
Aaron says, I'd like to be close enough to the zoo that I can stand on my roof and throw

947
00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:04.320
a banana and watch a monkey catch it in its mouth.

948
00:44:04.960 --> 00:44:06.000
I am speechless.

949
00:44:06.260 --> 00:44:07.300
I am speechless.

950
00:44:07.580 --> 00:44:10.100
This is her hope for Tallahassee?

951
00:44:10.240 --> 00:44:14.000
Well, this is her criteria for living anywhere.

952
00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:16.320
I guess so, living in a state capitol.

953
00:44:16.540 --> 00:44:16.740
Yeah.

954
00:44:17.320 --> 00:44:20.840
So I was curious, does Tallahassee have a zoo?

955
00:44:21.320 --> 00:44:24.980
Aaron is out of luck, but it does have 17 museuMs.

956
00:44:25.480 --> 00:44:25.700
Oh.

957
00:44:26.180 --> 00:44:31.740
You can go to witchmuseum.com, W-H-I-C-H, witchmuseum.com.

958
00:44:31.780 --> 00:44:31.980
Okay.

959
00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:35.020
To learn more about all the museums in Tallahassee.

960
00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:41.000
Also, Jenna, I did find there is a Tallahassee museum that has animal exhibits, a ropes course,

961
00:44:41.080 --> 00:44:42.060
and zip lining.

962
00:44:42.340 --> 00:44:44.980
It's not a zoo, but maybe that's where Aaron would have landed.

963
00:44:45.160 --> 00:44:45.640
Maybe so.

964
00:44:45.780 --> 00:44:45.980
Yeah.

965
00:44:45.980 --> 00:44:47.700
I'm a big museum person.

966
00:44:47.900 --> 00:44:48.220
I know.

967
00:44:48.320 --> 00:44:50.220
When I go to cities, I love a museum.

968
00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:53.940
One of my favorite places in the United States is Santa Fe, New Mexico.

969
00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:58.140
They have so much history stuff.

970
00:44:58.140 --> 00:44:59.000
We were just there.

971
00:44:59.120 --> 00:45:00.740
We went to Bandolier National Monument.

972
00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:04.640
I highly recommend a trip to Santa Fe, New Mexico.

973
00:45:04.640 --> 00:45:06.360
It's a hidden gem of the United States.

974
00:45:06.360 --> 00:45:08.780
And if I ever go to Florida, I'm going to check out these museuMs.

975
00:45:09.260 --> 00:45:12.260
I mean, I feel like Tallahassee has a lot of cool things to see.

976
00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:14.740
I think Aaron's going to do well there.

977
00:45:14.900 --> 00:45:15.180
She will.

978
00:45:15.180 --> 00:45:17.620
If she can give up the weird monkey banana dream.

979
00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:19.220
Yeah, because she's not going to find that.

980
00:45:19.220 --> 00:45:20.040
She's not going to get that.

981
00:45:20.500 --> 00:45:24.180
Well, back to the episode, this human pyramid is not going to go well for Dwight.

982
00:45:24.300 --> 00:45:25.760
He's going to end up on a stretcher.

983
00:45:26.060 --> 00:45:26.920
It's an emergency.

984
00:45:26.920 --> 00:45:28.400
He needs his appendix out.

985
00:45:29.080 --> 00:45:34.300
Back at Dunder Mifflin Scranton, Andy is really leveling up his reception game.

986
00:45:34.480 --> 00:45:35.720
He's made many pizzas.

987
00:45:36.520 --> 00:45:37.660
They're very popular.

988
00:45:38.480 --> 00:45:40.620
And he's faxing up a storm.

989
00:45:40.940 --> 00:45:43.900
He's shredding and faxing at the same time.

990
00:45:44.100 --> 00:45:45.900
Yeah, he says, Daryl, awesome news.

991
00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:46.860
Your fax went through.

992
00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:48.480
And Daryl says, oh, f*** yeah.

993
00:45:49.680 --> 00:45:51.060
I had to know.

994
00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:53.600
I had to know if this was scripted.

995
00:45:53.640 --> 00:45:55.840
And it was verbatim.

996
00:45:55.960 --> 00:45:56.400
