He doesn't want to get his taxes at home?

1248
00:56:45.600 --> 00:56:46.460
It's weird.

1249
00:56:46.460 --> 00:56:48.880
That is a weird detail that was missed on me.

1250
00:56:49.760 --> 00:56:55.280
Next up is a scene that I found odd, and I have a question for you about it.

1251
00:56:55.420 --> 00:56:55.620
Okay.

1252
00:56:56.040 --> 00:57:01.820
Pam and Daryl approach Andy at reception, and they're, like, trying to talk Andy out of being

1253
00:57:01.820 --> 00:57:07.340
the receptionist, and it didn't really make sense to me because, you know, he's doing

1254
00:57:07.340 --> 00:57:11.540
a really, really good job, and Pam didn't want to do the job anyway.

1255
00:57:12.620 --> 00:57:18.320
So I actually went to the script, and I saw that there was originally this storyline where

1256
00:57:18.320 --> 00:57:20.720
Andy is not doing a good job.

1257
00:57:20.860 --> 00:57:27.160
He's kind of ignoring his job as manager because he gets so distracted by reception, and people

1258
00:57:27.160 --> 00:57:29.160
are really frustrated, particularly Kelly.

1259
00:57:29.420 --> 00:57:34.780
And that's why Pam and Daryl have this scene where they're like, Andy, it's time to give

1260
00:57:34.780 --> 00:57:35.380
up reception.

1261
00:57:35.380 --> 00:57:35.840
Yeah.

1262
00:57:36.080 --> 00:57:37.640
Is that in deleted scenes at all?

1263
00:57:37.760 --> 00:57:38.440
Did we shoot it?

1264
00:57:38.540 --> 00:57:39.100
A little bit.

1265
00:57:39.360 --> 00:57:43.480
Basically, there's a storyline with Kelly and Creed where Kelly says she was promised a

1266
00:57:43.480 --> 00:57:43.820
raise.

1267
00:57:43.900 --> 00:57:44.120
Uh-huh.

1268
00:57:44.460 --> 00:57:46.820
And Andy goes, I never promised you a raise.

1269
00:57:46.820 --> 00:57:49.380
And she said, the manager before you did.

1270
00:57:49.740 --> 00:57:50.980
And Creed goes, that's right.

1271
00:57:50.980 --> 00:57:52.100
I promised her a raise.

1272
00:57:52.100 --> 00:57:52.860
Yeah, that's right.

1273
00:57:53.040 --> 00:57:54.420
He also promised himself a raise.

1274
00:57:54.460 --> 00:57:55.000
That's right.

1275
00:57:55.360 --> 00:57:58.000
And Andy's like, we don't have any money for raises, guys.

1276
00:57:58.140 --> 00:57:58.900
We don't have any money.

1277
00:57:59.020 --> 00:58:01.400
And then Phyllis goes, does that mean I'm not getting my raise?

1278
00:58:01.500 --> 00:58:04.800
And he's like, Phyllis, I told you not to say anything about that.

1279
00:58:04.940 --> 00:58:11.840
So he had given Phyllis a raise, but isn't going to honor Creed's raises.

1280
00:58:12.120 --> 00:58:12.360
Yeah.

1281
00:58:12.820 --> 00:58:20.080
And Kelly is pissed off the whole entire episode, demanding that Andy answer for this the whole

1282
00:58:20.080 --> 00:58:20.540
episode.

1283
00:58:20.540 --> 00:58:23.800
And he's kind of hiding out at reception.

1284
00:58:24.020 --> 00:58:24.180
Yes.

1285
00:58:24.400 --> 00:58:25.300
So he doesn't have to deal with it.

1286
00:58:25.300 --> 00:58:25.720
That's right.

1287
00:58:25.840 --> 00:58:28.060
There's scenes where Kelly and Creed go up to talk to him.

1288
00:58:28.120 --> 00:58:29.480
And he's like, oh, sorry, I got to take this call.

1289
00:58:29.580 --> 00:58:30.700
Oh, I got to do this fax.

1290
00:58:30.820 --> 00:58:34.360
And he just keeps punting this conversation with Kelly and Creed.

1291
00:58:34.580 --> 00:58:34.820
OK.

1292
00:58:35.200 --> 00:58:35.720
Got it.

1293
00:58:36.400 --> 00:58:40.960
So Andy, I guess, does reflect and think, OK, maybe I shouldn't be the receptioniSt.

1294
00:58:41.040 --> 00:58:45.220
But I do want to point out that there's a month of no one answering phones now.

1295
00:58:45.400 --> 00:58:46.360
I guess so.

1296
00:58:46.680 --> 00:58:47.980
What's going to happen next week?

1297
00:58:47.980 --> 00:58:48.720
I'm so curious.

1298
00:58:48.880 --> 00:58:49.880
Who's going to answer the phone?

1299
00:58:49.880 --> 00:58:50.560
I don't know.

1300
00:58:50.780 --> 00:58:56.380
He does take a call from a client who misses Aaron, and he realizes he misses her, too.

1301
00:58:56.620 --> 00:58:56.960
Mm-hmm.

1302
00:58:57.140 --> 00:58:57.720
I'm sorry.

1303
00:58:57.800 --> 00:58:58.660
I like him with Jessica.

1304
00:58:58.900 --> 00:58:59.560
I know you do.

1305
00:58:59.780 --> 00:59:02.200
Listen, I'm rooting for Jessica as well.

1306
00:59:02.200 --> 00:59:05.360
Back in Tallahassee, Dwight has a talking head.

1307
00:59:05.500 --> 00:59:15.260
He said he had two goals today, to make himself a clear candidate for vice president, which he believes he achieved, and to find a memorable souvenir for Philip,

1308
00:59:15.520 --> 00:59:17.520
which he achieved thanks to his appendix.

1309
00:59:18.240 --> 00:59:18.720
Yes.

1310
00:59:19.160 --> 00:59:29.580
We had a fan question from Olivia Oh in Ireland who said, I was wondering, when Dwight holds up his appendix as a souvenir to give Philip, what was used as the fake appendix?

1311
00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:33.680
It looked so realistically gross, and it made me think of who had to put that together.

1312
00:59:34.100 --> 00:59:35.520
You know Phil Shea did.

1313
00:59:35.520 --> 00:59:41.280
He did, and I actually was very curious, what was in the bag?

1314
00:59:41.400 --> 00:59:48.180
Like, what happens when Phil Shea reads a script and he realizes that he has to come up with an appendix in a baggie?

1315
00:59:48.960 --> 00:59:51.340
So, lady, I reached out and asked him.

1316
00:59:51.620 --> 00:59:52.780
Poor Phil Shea.

1317
00:59:53.000 --> 00:59:55.060
These are the things we text him about.

1318
00:59:55.460 --> 01:00:03.520
He said that he rented this fake appendix from a makeup and body parts special effects company called Matthew Mungle.

1319
01:00:03.840 --> 01:00:04.460
Hold up.

1320
01:00:04.740 --> 01:00:05.000
Yep.

1321
01:00:05.240 --> 01:00:07.580
You can rent a fake appendix?

1322
01:00:07.720 --> 01:00:11.620
You can rent all kinds of fake body parts from Matthew Mungle.

1323
01:00:11.780 --> 01:00:12.440
Oh, my Lord.

1324
01:00:12.720 --> 01:00:13.000
Yeah.

1325
01:00:13.420 --> 01:00:14.600
But it gets better.

1326
01:00:14.600 --> 01:00:25.860
Phil said they actually had a few appendix to choose from, so he presented them, probably on a silver tray, to our writer Danny and director Matt Sohn,

1327
01:00:25.880 --> 01:00:27.400
and they picked the one they liked.

1328
01:00:27.840 --> 01:00:29.060
Oh, my gosh.

1329
01:00:29.360 --> 01:00:33.260
Phil said that when he reads a script, he breaks it down into categories.

1330
01:00:33.600 --> 01:00:36.700
The categories are rentals, purchases, and design.

1331
01:00:37.560 --> 01:00:40.580
He said also he looks for any promo opportunities.

1332
01:00:40.580 --> 01:00:45.060
Is there any way to get a company to give them money if that helps?

1333
01:00:45.060 --> 01:00:45.620
Product placement.

1334
01:00:45.800 --> 01:00:45.960
Yeah.

1335
01:00:46.840 --> 01:00:52.040
Also, he said if something does need to be designed, he has subcategories.

1336
01:00:52.200 --> 01:00:55.820
Is it something they can make in-house or something they have to source elsewhere?

1337
01:00:56.260 --> 01:00:58.740
And he only ever has a week to get it all done.

1338
01:00:59.200 --> 01:01:02.940
That's just fascinating to me that he pulls that off every week.

1339
01:01:03.080 --> 01:01:03.360
I know.

1340
01:01:03.460 --> 01:01:04.380
It's just amazing.

1341
01:01:04.540 --> 01:01:04.800
Amazing.

1342
01:01:04.800 --> 01:01:09.700
So thank you, Phil, for giving me the lowdown on how you got a fake appendix.

1343
01:01:10.620 --> 01:01:14.120
This episode is going to end with a very special gueSt.

1344
01:01:14.380 --> 01:01:18.140
Nellie brings in Wally Amos of Famous Amos Cookies.

1345
01:01:18.380 --> 01:01:18.780
Mm-hmm.

1346
01:01:19.240 --> 01:01:23.300
As he starts his speech, all anyone can think about is, are there cookies?

1347
01:01:23.480 --> 01:01:24.300
Can I eat them now?

1348
01:01:24.480 --> 01:01:25.440
Can we open the cookies?

1349
01:01:25.660 --> 01:01:27.420
They do not want his motivational speech.

1350
01:01:27.500 --> 01:01:27.700
No.

1351
01:01:27.780 --> 01:01:28.760
They just want the cookies.

1352
01:01:29.200 --> 01:01:33.240
Everyone, this was the real Wally Amos of Famous Amos Cookies.

1353
01:01:33.240 --> 01:01:38.680
He was the first and only person ever scripted for this bit at the end of the episode.

1354
01:01:38.980 --> 01:01:40.800
He is originally from Florida.

1355
01:01:41.540 --> 01:01:46.820
Steve Burgess says that Allison Jones contacted his reps and he was interested, so we got to

1356
01:01:46.820 --> 01:01:47.440
make it happen.

1357
01:01:47.680 --> 01:01:53.780
It is a little complicated, though, because he is no longer associated with Famous Amos Cookies,

1358
01:01:54.000 --> 01:01:55.660
the cookie that he invented.

1359
01:01:55.940 --> 01:01:57.520
His story is fascinating.

1360
01:01:57.780 --> 01:01:59.200
It has a lot of ups and downs.

1361
01:01:59.680 --> 01:02:00.880
I'm going to tell it to you.

1362
01:02:00.880 --> 01:02:05.760
So, he no longer has Famous Amos Cookies and he's...

1363
01:02:05.760 --> 01:02:06.660
Famous Amos.

1364
01:02:07.020 --> 01:02:07.500
Okay.

1365
01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:08.420
Yes.

1366
01:02:08.640 --> 01:02:09.340
What happened?

1367
01:02:09.580 --> 01:02:11.540
So, first of all, he was born in Florida.

1368
01:02:11.820 --> 01:02:16.280
He eventually moved to Los Angeles and got a job in the mailroom at the William Morris

1369
01:02:16.280 --> 01:02:17.020
Talent Agency.

1370
01:02:17.280 --> 01:02:22.500
He worked his way up from the mailroom to become the first Black talent agent in the industry.

1371
01:02:23.160 --> 01:02:25.540
He was the head of the rock and roll department.

1372
01:02:25.780 --> 01:02:27.800
He signed Simon and Garfunkel.

1373
01:02:27.800 --> 01:02:31.700
He worked with people like Diana Ross, Sam Cook, and Dionne Warwick.

1374
01:02:31.840 --> 01:02:32.200
Wow.

1375
01:02:32.480 --> 01:02:36.800
But while he was working there, he was just getting sort of disillusioned with show business.

1376
01:02:37.060 --> 01:02:41.140
And so, to relieve stress, he started baking in his free time.

1377
01:02:41.340 --> 01:02:44.900
And he would make his aunt's cookie recipes and bring them to meetings.

1378
01:02:45.740 --> 01:02:47.060
And people loved them.

1379
01:02:47.400 --> 01:02:51.040
Like, people started saying, will you bring cookies to the meeting?

1380
01:02:51.940 --> 01:02:58.780
So, in 1975, he opened a cookie store on Sunset Boulevard and he only sold three kinds of

1381
01:02:58.780 --> 01:03:03.900
cookies, chocolate chip peanut butter, chocolate chip with pecan, and butterscotch chip with

1382
01:03:03.900 --> 01:03:04.280
pecan.

1383
01:03:04.820 --> 01:03:06.960
They were a zero-preservative cookie.

1384
01:03:07.300 --> 01:03:14.060
Basically, the first craft cookie, the first, like, gourmet cookie shop, it was a huge hit.

1385
01:03:14.060 --> 01:03:15.520
He quit being a talent agent.

1386
01:03:15.700 --> 01:03:18.780
By 1982, he was making, like, $12 million in revenue.

1387
01:03:18.780 --> 01:03:22.640
And he became famous as Famous Amos Cookies.

1388
01:03:22.880 --> 01:03:23.200
Wow.

1389
01:03:23.400 --> 01:03:26.260
He started appearing on television shows as himself.

1390
01:03:26.540 --> 01:03:32.640
But eventually, I guess there started to be some trouble financially with the company.

1391
01:03:33.000 --> 01:03:34.600
And he sold it.

1392
01:03:35.040 --> 01:03:37.920
And then it was resold and resold.

1393
01:03:37.920 --> 01:03:43.980
And eventually, the owners of the company started adding shelf-stable ingredients to it.

1394
01:03:44.040 --> 01:03:47.660
And they started mass-producing them and putting them in grocery stores.

1395
01:03:47.700 --> 01:03:49.720
And they were a huge success.

1396
01:03:50.320 --> 01:03:56.800
But Amos was not cashing in on any of this because when he sold the cookie, he sold his name.

1397
01:03:57.320 --> 01:03:58.740
He sold everything.

1398
01:03:58.740 --> 01:04:00.480
He sold all the rights to it.

1399
01:04:01.500 --> 01:04:07.000
So, he tried to launch a new hazelnut cookie as Wally Amos Presents.

1400
01:04:07.420 --> 01:04:15.620
And that company that owned Famous Amos sued him because he had put the name Wally Amos on his new cookies.

1401
01:04:15.620 --> 01:04:16.620
And they won!

1402
01:04:16.980 --> 01:04:20.440
He was told he could never put his name on another cookie again.

1403
01:04:20.720 --> 01:04:21.840
That's horrible.

1404
01:04:22.200 --> 01:04:22.700
Horrible.

1405
01:04:22.980 --> 01:04:26.900
So, he launched another cookie called Uncle Noname.

1406
01:04:28.160 --> 01:04:29.440
Like, no name.

1407
01:04:30.380 --> 01:04:30.820
Noname.

1408
01:04:31.060 --> 01:04:32.840
Is it pronounced Noname?

1409
01:04:33.120 --> 01:04:36.220
Well, it's spelled N-O-N-A-M-E.

1410
01:04:36.380 --> 01:04:39.480
And the E has a little, like, you know, thing on it.

1411
01:04:39.560 --> 01:04:39.780
Okay.

1412
01:04:39.840 --> 01:04:40.920
Like the French do.

1413
01:04:41.140 --> 01:04:41.400
Okay.

1414
01:04:41.620 --> 01:04:43.240
So, I assume it's called Noname.

1415
01:04:43.460 --> 01:04:43.720
Okay.

1416
01:04:43.720 --> 01:04:44.840
Maybe it's called No Name.

1417
01:04:45.780 --> 01:04:47.180
Well, I didn't know the spelling.

1418
01:04:47.360 --> 01:04:47.560
Okay.

1419
01:04:47.720 --> 01:04:49.940
So, now we have Uncle Noname.

1420
01:04:50.400 --> 01:04:50.840
Cookies.

1421
01:04:51.240 --> 01:04:52.460
Those did not work out.

1422
01:04:53.140 --> 01:04:59.460
Finally, in 1999, he signed on to be a spokesperson for the Famous Amos cookie.

1423
01:04:59.720 --> 01:05:01.500
The cookie he invented.

1424
01:05:02.740 --> 01:05:11.920
But, like, he was just, like, imagine if you invented Verizon Wireless, but all you could do was be the spokesperson for Verizon.

1425
01:05:11.920 --> 01:05:13.540
You were just, like, the guy on the commercial.

1426
01:05:13.540 --> 01:05:16.880
So, still, he was getting no, like, revenue.

1427
01:05:17.020 --> 01:05:21.820
He was just, like, you know, the commercial guy.

1428
01:05:22.100 --> 01:05:24.460
Anyway, eventually, he moved to Hawaii.

1429
01:05:24.680 --> 01:05:26.320
He started a company selling muffins.

1430
01:05:26.320 --> 01:05:31.200
He wrote multiple books about his experiences, and he is a motivational speaker.

1431
01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:40.320
And his signature shirt and hat are on display at the Smithsonian National Museum of History, and we flew him in from Hawaii to do this episode.

1432
01:05:40.660 --> 01:05:44.500
And Famous Amos agreed to let us feature the cookies on the show.

1433
01:05:45.180 --> 01:05:49.040
But only if we bought real Famous Amos cookies, we couldn't have any props.

1434
01:05:50.360 --> 01:05:52.780
Isn't that, like, it's fascinating.

1435
01:05:53.100 --> 01:05:56.040
I hate that he lost the rights to his own name.

1436
01:05:56.040 --> 01:05:57.240
His own name.

1437
01:05:57.480 --> 01:05:57.960
Yes.

1438
01:05:58.240 --> 01:06:05.080
And I watched a bunch of videos of him as a motivational speaker because we got this fan question from Roz F.

1439
01:06:05.140 --> 01:06:06.600
in Washington, D.C., who said,

1440
01:06:06.720 --> 01:06:13.360
I've always felt bad that Wally Amos didn't get to deliver a motivational speech because I've heard him speak, and he's quite inspirational.

1441
01:06:13.660 --> 01:06:15.060
And Roz is correct.

1442
01:06:15.660 --> 01:06:17.960
So, yeah, what a story.

1443
01:06:18.240 --> 01:06:19.060
What a story.

1444
01:06:19.180 --> 01:06:21.000
I'm so glad to have learned all of that.

1445
01:06:21.140 --> 01:06:22.540
Yeah, I know.

1446
01:06:22.540 --> 01:06:26.500
I want to say something, but I don't want to get sued by Famous Amos people.

1447
01:06:26.780 --> 01:06:27.820
What do you want to say?

1448
01:06:28.980 --> 01:06:32.180
Well, there's only one Famous Amos, and that's Wally Amos.

1449
01:06:32.760 --> 01:06:34.240
I think you can say that.

1450
01:06:34.820 --> 01:06:36.620
Well, that was Tallahassee, everybody.

1451
01:06:36.620 --> 01:06:38.760
I had a lot of fun revisiting this episode.

1452
01:06:38.940 --> 01:06:39.200
Yes.

1453
01:06:39.340 --> 01:06:43.440
A big thank you to Steve Burgess and Phil Shea, who helped me fill in some blanks for this episode.

1454
01:06:43.780 --> 01:06:49.340
We won't be here next week because we'll be in Toronto, but when we come back, we have a lot to talk about.

1455
01:06:49.340 --> 01:06:50.820
It's After Hours.

1456
01:06:50.960 --> 01:06:51.460
Mm-hmm.

1457
01:06:51.740 --> 01:06:54.000
And a lot happens in After Hours.

1458
01:06:54.180 --> 01:06:54.900
It sure does.

1459
01:06:55.120 --> 01:06:56.500
We really get into it.

1460
01:06:56.740 --> 01:06:58.040
Have a great week, everyone.

1461
01:06:58.320 --> 01:06:58.940
See you later.

1462
01:07:01.110 --> 01:07:03.150
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.

